## C5 Scoping

**Scoping.** Protein in both directions: outbound per-field
narrowing, inbound acceptance, the tombstone rule, re-snapshot on widening,
mutable-predicate grants, honest empty states. One selector language.
**Surface:** the per-contact pairing panel already filed into this cluster —
what they may see of you, what you accept from them, per field. Scoping
nobody can inspect is scoping nobody can trust.

**Most of the mechanism is already built; the surface is the gap.**
`organ_contact.scope_fields` and `accept_fields` both exist, both are applied
through the one shared predicate, and `set-contact-scope` is a real Action. The
Organ sand only DISPLAYS the current scope and offers on/off chips for the whole
feed. There is no per-field editor, which is the thing an owner actually needs.

**Decided 2026-08-18, by the owner.**

**The field list is curated, and a new column is invisible until somebody adds
it.** The alternative — offering every column on `record` — leaks schema
vocabulary into the interface and grows with every migration, so the owner is
asked about fields that mean nothing to them. The cost is accepted and stated:
a migration that adds a shareable field is not finished until that field is in
the list, and until then it simply cannot be narrowed on. A field nobody can
choose is a smaller failure than a list nobody can read.

**A new contact starts narrowed to NOTHING, not unnarrowed.** Default-closed,
the same stance as the invite door: pairing is a decision to be reachable, not
a decision to be readable, and the two should not arrive together. **This
changes behaviour for pairings that already exist and that is deliberate** —
existing contacts are not grandfathered into a wider default just because they
were added before the default changed. The panel says what it is doing rather
than doing it silently, because a contact who suddenly sees nothing must be
explainable as a setting rather than discovered as a break.

**Row selection for a contact IS a Protein.** Not a new capability — Protein
already evaluates predicates and the fact bus already re-runs a query when a
committed fact might affect it. What a contact receives is the Protein you point
at them: named Records, or a Concept, or a kind, or any other filter the one
selector language already has. Sync consumes the result the way any subscriber
does.

**A rule may select on MUTABLE state, and that is in this cluster** — moved
here 2026-08-18 from its own deferral. Mutable state is anything about a Record
that can change after it exists: a Concept on it, a quantity, a status. A rule
over it — "everything tagged family", "every task not yet done" — answers
differently over time without anybody editing the rule, so each Record entering
is a GRANT and each one leaving is a REVOKE, fired by a change that was not an
act of sharing.

**It costs nothing extra to allow, because the design above already is it.**
The old deferral said this needed a capability that did not exist, and that was
true of a world where row selection was a hide LIST rather than a query. Once
selection is a Protein and membership is recomputed per changed Record, asking
"does this Record match now, and did it before" is the same question whether the
field it turns on is mutable or not. Restricting rules to immutable fields would
throw away the useful half and simplify nothing.

**The guardrail is a view, and it ships BEFORE the feature, not after.** A rule
must be able to answer "what are you sharing right now" before it can be
switched on. Tagging a Record for an unrelated reason silently sharing it is the
failure this feature makes possible, and the membership table already holds the
answer, so this is a read rather than new work. A rule nobody can inspect is how
people overshare — the same reason this cluster's surface line exists at all.

**Membership is MATERIALISED per contact, and that is what makes entry O(1).**
A row per `(contact, record)` currently shared. The fact bus already says which
Record changed, so the work on a commit is "does this one Record match this
contact's Protein now, and did it before" — one evaluation, one insert or one
delete. Diffing whole result sets would be O(set) on every commit and was the
obvious wrong shape. The table pays for itself twice more: it is what the
"what is this rule sharing right now" view reads, and it is how we know what a
contact holds when a Record later leaves.

**Entry catches the peer up, and how is an implementation question rather than
a design one** (owner, 2026-08-18: as performant as it can be, the method is
not the owner's call). What must be true is that the Record arrives COMPLETE:
every op for it already sits below the peer's catch-up marker, so with no
replay it arrives as an empty shell. Reuse the replay-by-identity that widening
a field scope already performs, bounded by that one Record's history rather
than by the log. Measure it against a Record with a long collab history before
calling it done — a snapshot plus tail is available if the replay is the wrong
cost, and that choice is free to make later because nothing else depends on it.

**Deleting here deletes there — decided 2026-08-18. This is ordinary deletion
and needs no special case.** The rule is one sentence: **a tombstone follows
whoever was sent the Record.** They hold it, so they are told it is gone;
otherwise a copy nobody can clean up lives on their disk forever. A contact who
was never sent it never hears about it, because a tombstone for a Record they
never had would be telling them it existed.

The materialised membership above is what makes that answerable — it says who
was sent what, so "did they have it" is a lookup rather than a guess. The
question does not need to ask whether a Record is hidden NOW, or whether it has
left the set since; those cases both fall out of the one sentence. The OLD
hide-list rule withheld the delete of a hidden Record on the reasoning that
sending it would reveal the Record existed. That reasoning survives exactly
where it was right — for a Record they were never sent — and stops being a
separate rule.

**The hide list becomes a CLAUSE of the Protein, not a second gate — decided
2026-08-18.** Today's `visibility_rule` exception list is well made and cheap:
usually empty, checked empty-first, loaded once per feed as a set, and an O(1)
lookup for `record` ops. But cheapness was never the question. It answers the
same question the Protein answers — does this contact receive this Record — and
two mechanisms that can answer it differently is precisely what the shared
`op_in_scope` predicate exists to prevent; the second copy is the one that
leaks, and nothing fails until it does. It also defaults the opposite way
(shared-unless-excepted), which is the default we just replaced.

So it survives as `<the contact's Protein> AND NOT hidden`, evaluated in one
place. Keeping it matters for the gesture rather than the mechanism: "stop
sending this one thing" has to stay one click, and making somebody edit a
predicate to hide a single Record would be a worse interface justified by a
tidier diagram.

**Rate-limiting caps the RECORDING, never the sync.** A flooding contact keeps
being processed at full speed; what stops is writing new rows into the ring.
Slowing the connection and refusing it were both considered and both refused:
either one lets a contact get itself ignored by sending garbage, which turns a
peer's choice into what looks like our bug. Capping the recording has nothing to
queue and therefore nothing to catch up on — it is current by construction.
The honesty cost is paid on the panel: it shows the count that was refused, not
only the rows that were kept, so "1,482 refused in the last hour, showing the
most recent 200" is what a person reads rather than a list that quietly stopped
growing.
